      "confidence": "Medium",
      "cwe_id": [
        89
      ],
      "note": "Same single root cause as the Entry.text_search_rank warning above, and the same escaping. Entry.excludes_only? asks PostgreSQL whether the parsed query matches the empty document, to refuse a pure-negative search such as \"-oil\" before it forces a sequential scan of the shared entries table. The interpolated fragment is the private Entry.tsquery_sql, which returns websearch_to_tsquery('english', <connection.quote'd literal>); Brakeman flags the interpolation because it cannot see through that private_class_method. Verified against the running database: Entry.excludes_only?(%q{x'; DROP TABLE entries; --}) returns false and the entries table survives. Unlike the ORDER BY case this statement could take a real $1 bind, and that was implemented and measured -- it does silence Brakeman -- but it was reverted because it forces websearch_to_tsquery('english', ...) to be written a second time, and the guard is only correct while it parses the query with the identical parser and text search configuration the search itself uses. One definition with a documented ignore was preferred over two definitions that can drift apart silently. If tsquery_sql ever stops routing the query through connection.quote, this ignore must go."
    },
